      "id": "kraftwerk-radioactivity-g1",
      "type": "background",
      "headline": "The song that became an anti-nuclear protest anthem",
      "snippet": "In 1991, Kraftwerk re-recorded \"Radioactivity\" for their remix album The Mix, turning it into a very different, openly anti-nuclear song. The new version dropped every reference to radio. It made \"stop radioactivity\" the central hook and added lyrics about a \"contaminated population.\" It also named Chernobyl, Harrisburg, Sellafield and Hiroshima. It was issued as a single with remixes by François Kevorkian and William Orbit.",
      "context": "The 1991 version was issued as a single with remixes by François Kevorkian and William Orbit.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "platform": "wikipedia"
      },
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactivity_(song)",
      "sourceDate": "2026-09-20"
    },
    {
      "id": "kraftwerk-radioactivity-g2",
      "type": "background",
      "headline": "Morse code hides lyrics inside the track",
      "snippet": "The original 1975 recording of Kraftwerk's \"Radioactivity\" has Morse code signals woven into it. Near the start they spell out the word radioactivity. Later iterations carry lines that mirror the English lyrics, such as \"discovered by Madame Curie\" and \"Radioactivity tune into the.\" The band's name, \"Kraftwerk\", is sent in Morse as the final signal, signing off the song.",
      "context": "The Morse iterations mirror the English lyrics.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "platform": "wikipedia"
      },
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactivity_(song)",
      "sourceDate": "2026-09-20"
    },
